# What never reaches a build context — and therefore never reaches an image
# layer, which is the part that matters: a file deleted in a later instruction
# is still in the layer that added it.
#
# Every pattern that must match a nested directory carries `**/`. Without it
# `node_modules/` matches the repository root and nothing else, which is how the
# host's macOS-compiled `node_modules` were reaching `COPY apps ./apps` — into a
# Linux image, on top of an install that had just resolved the right ones.

# Dependencies. Resolved inside the image, for the image's own platform.
**/node_modules

# Build output. Every Dockerfile compiles from source in its build stage; a
# `dist/` from the host is at best redundant and at worst a stale artefact
# nobody rebuilt.
**/dist
**/.output
**/.nuxt
**/.nitro
**/.data
**/build

# Caches and reports. None of them is an input to anything.
**/.turbo
**/coverage
**/*.log
**/.pnpm-store
**/tmp

# History. `GIT_COMMIT` arrives as a build argument instead, so no image needs
# the repository to know which commit it came from.
.git
.github

# Credentials. `.env` files hold real values on a developer's machine, and the
# acceptance criterion is that no image layer contains a secret.
**/.env
**/.env.*

# Docker's own files: an image does not need the instructions that built it.
Dockerfile*
**/Dockerfile*
.dockerignore
infra/docker/

# Editor and OS noise.
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.vscode
.idea
